---
description: Refactoring and change quality — KISS, DRY, SOLID; no TS/type bypass.
alwaysApply: true
---

When refactoring, apply KISS, DRY, SOLID, CLEAN. Avoid overengineering like hell. Disregard backwards compatibility. Do the "proper thing".

When proposing a change, RECHECK YOUR LOGIC: would these changes make sense? Would they break adjacent systems? Explore the long-term consequences of your solution.

Never ship unresolved TypeScript errors — `pnpm build` is the type gate (there is no separate typecheck script). Avoid `any` and type-check bypasses (`@ts-expect-error`, `@ts-ignore`); prefer `unknown`, generics, and narrowing. ESLint does not enforce this uniformly (`api/.eslintrc.js` disables `@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any`; `web/eslint.config.js` sets it to `warn`) — still do not introduce new `any` in new or refactored code unless strictly necessary.
